# mariadb-mcp-launcher — the extensionless name Codex resolves, on every OS.
#
# This file exists so that ONE `command` in .mcp.json works on macOS, Linux and
# Windows alike. Codex stores a plugin's .mcp.json `command` verbatim, expanding
# no placeholder (there is no ${CODEX_PLUGIN_ROOT}, and ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT} is
# not expanded on the MCP spawn path), so the command has to be *relative* and
# paired with `"cwd": "."`, which Codex does resolve to the plugin root. But a
# relative command still cannot name one file that both OS families can execute:
# Windows cannot run a .sh, and .mcp.json cannot branch per OS.
#
# An extensionless name resolves that, because of how Codex resolves the program
# (codex-rs/rmcp-client/src/program_resolver.rs):
#
#   * On Unix it hands the name to the OS unchanged, and the kernel runs THIS
#     file via the shebang below — the extension is meaningless here.
#   * On Windows it resolves through the `which` crate, which walks %PATHEXT%
#     and lands on mariadb-mcp-launcher.cmd. This file is skipped there: having
#     no extension, it is tested with GetBinaryType(), which rejects a text file.
#
# The same mechanism is why `npx`/`pnpm`/`yarn` work in a Codex MCP entry.
#
# Keeping this a shim rather than renaming mariadb-mcp-launcher.sh keeps that
# file's name identical across all the plugins in this repo, where it is also
# referenced by the Claude and OpenCode configs and by the setup scripts.
exec "$(dirname "$0")/mariadb-mcp-launcher.sh" "$@"
